            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "header",
          "index": 1,
          "bbox": [
            0.185,
            0.069,
            0.488,
            0.086
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Ganita Manjari | Grade 9 | Part I"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.108,
            0.884,
            0.148
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "the amount paid by a player increases by `50 for every additional match played. Such patterns are called "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "linear patterns",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.148,
            0.884,
            0.187
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "When we equate a linear polynomial in one variable to a constant, we get a "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "linear equation",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": ". Let us consider the following example."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.19,
            0.885,
            0.228
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Example 6:",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " The sum of two numbers is 64. One of the numbers is 10 more than the other. What are the two numbers?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.228,
            0.885,
            0.324
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Let the smaller number be x. Then the larger number must be "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "x + 1 0 ,"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " Since their sum is 64, we have the linear equation "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "x + (x + 10) = 64"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " This implies that "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "2x + 10 = 64"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " . Note that "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "2 x \\pm 1 0"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " is a linear polynomial. By equating it to 64 we get a linear equation 2x = 54 or x = 27. The numbers are therefore, 27 and 37."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.324,
            0.885,
            0.402
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Polynomials can also be thought of as input-output processes. For instance, consider the linear polynomial 2x + 3. For every x, there is a corresponding value of the polynomial 2x + 3. For instance, if "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "x = 4"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " we substitute 4 in the expression to get "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "2 \\times 4 + 3 = 11"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.401,
            0.88,
            0.42
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "If x = –6, then we substitute –6 in the expression to get "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "2x - 6 + 3 = - 9"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.421,
            0.887,
            0.499
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Fig. 2.3 shows this process as an input-output machine where the input is the value of x and the output is the value of 2x + 3. This process can be referred to as a "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "function",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " where the expression 2x + 3 is a function of the variable x. You will learn more about functions in later grades."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.303,
            0.5,
            0.686,
            0.707
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 9,
              "bbox": [
                0.303,
                0.5,
                0.686,
                0.707
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_4_image_9.jpg",
              "content": ""
            },
            {
              "type": "image_caption",
              "index": 10,
              "bbox": [
                0.257,
                0.717,
                0.738,
                0.735
              ],
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Fig. 2.3: A linear expression as an input-output process"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.132,
            0.758,
            0.399,
            0.781
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Think and Reflect"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.128,
            0.795,
            0.868,
            0.91
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "We have learnt that to evaluate the value of an algebraic expression, we substitute a value of the variable in the given expression. Consider Example 3, where the wire is bent to form a rectangle. Here, the area of the rectangle, "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "1 0 x   -   x ^ { 2 } ,"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " is a function of x. Can you interpret this as an input-output process? What value does the expression take when "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "x = 6    cm ?"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
